  "text": "Globally, TB is a major public health issue with high morbidity and mortality. It is estimated that millions of new cases arise each year, particularly in regions with limited healthcare resources, where factors like poverty, malnutrition, and the prevalence of HIV/AIDS further exacerbate the situation [17, 18]. In this study, treatment outcome and factors associated with TB were assessed. The magnitude of successful treatment outcomes among TB patients was found to be 86.9%, which is a bit lower than the world (88%) and African (88%) average TB treatment success rate for new and relapse cases reported in 2024 [1]. However, this result is nearly similar to studies conducted in Gibe Woreda, Southern Ethiopia (85.5%) [11] and Debre Tabor Town, Northwestern Ethiopia (87.1%) [19]. Such consistency in outcomes could suggest comparable healthcare practices or disease management strategies in these regions (Table 4).",
